00:00:36.953 --> 00:00:39.937
You may have heard that computers work on one side.

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:41.737
ones and zeros.

00:00:41.737 --> 00:00:45.737
Or you may have seen scary looking visuals like this,

00:00:45.737 --> 00:00:48.657
but almost nobody today actually deals directly

00:00:48.657 --> 00:00:49.897
with these ones and zeros.

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:54.922
But ones and zeros do play a big role in how computers work on the inside.

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:01.907
all the information in a computer.

00:01:01.907 --> 00:01:05.907
How do you store or represent information using electricity?

00:01:05.907 --> 00:01:09.891
Well, if you have a single wire with electricity flowing through it...

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:17.091
the signal can either be on or off. That's not a lot of choices, but it's a really important start.

00:01:17.091 --> 00:01:19.651
With one wire we can represent a yes.

00:01:19.876 --> 00:01:28.136
or no, true or false, a 1 or a 0, or anything else with only two options.

00:01:28.136 --> 00:01:29.836
This on-off state of a single state of a single state of a single state of a single state

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.940
wire is called a bit and it's the smallest piece of information a computer

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:39.700
can store. If you use more wires you get more bits more ones and zeros with more

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:39.845
bits

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:42.845
you could represent more complex information.

00:01:42.845 --> 00:01:47.845
But to understand that, we need to learn about something called the binary number system.

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:57.909
In the decimal number system, we have 10 digits from 0 to 9, and that's how we've all learned

00:01:57.909 --> 00:01:58.909
to count.

00:01:58.909 --> 00:01:59.814
In the binary, we have 10 digits from 0 to 9, and that's how we've all learned to

00:01:59.814 --> 00:02:06.954
binary number system, we only have two digits, 0 and 1. With these two digits, we can count

00:02:06.954 --> 00:02:09.274
up to any number.

00:02:09.274 --> 00:02:09.799
Here's how this works.

00:02:09.799 --> 00:02:15.799
In the decimal number system we're all used to, each position in a number has a different value.

00:02:15.799 --> 00:02:19.783
There's the 1 position, the 10 position, the 100% position.

00:02:19.783 --> 00:02:25.543
position and so on. For example, a 9 in the 100 position is a 900.

00:02:26.903 --> 00:02:29.743
In binary, each position also carries a value.

00:02:29.768 --> 00:02:34.888
But instead of multiplying by 10 each time, we multiply by 2.

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:39.728
So there is the 1's position, the 2's position, the 4's position, the 8's position.

00:02:39.752 --> 00:02:42.232
position, and so on.

00:02:42.232 --> 00:02:48.512
For example, the number 9 in binary is 1001.

00:02:48.512 --> 00:02:49.737
To calculate the value

00:02:49.737 --> 00:02:57.777
we add 1 times 8 plus 0 times 4 plus 0 times 2 plus 1 times 1.

00:02:57.777 --> 00:02:59.617
Almost nobody does this math because

00:02:59.721 --> 00:03:01.161
computer do it for us.

00:03:02.641 --> 00:03:05.541
What's important is that any number can be represented

00:03:05.541 --> 00:03:07.881
with only ones and zeros,

00:03:07.881 --> 00:03:09.706
or by a bunch of wires that are on.

00:03:09.706 --> 00:03:11.466
on or off.

00:03:11.466 --> 00:03:12.726
The more wires you use,

00:03:12.726 --> 00:03:15.066
the larger the numbers you can store.

00:03:15.066 --> 00:03:16.346
- With eight wires,

00:03:16.346 --> 00:03:19.691
you can store numbers between zero and...

00:03:19.691 --> 00:03:29.451
255. That's eight wants. With just 32 wires, you can store all the way from zero to over 4 billion.

00:03:29.675 --> 00:03:34.675
Using the binary number system, you can represent any number you like.

00:03:34.675 --> 00:03:37.675
But what about other types of information?

00:03:37.675 --> 00:03:39.660
Like text, images,

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:45.540
or sound. It turns out that all these things can also be represented with numbers.

00:03:49.644 --> 00:03:53.184
Think of all the letters in the alphabet.

00:03:53.184 --> 00:03:55.184
You could assign a number to each letter.

00:03:55.184 --> 00:03:58.704
A could be one, B could be two, and so on.

00:03:58.704 --> 00:03:59.624
You can then represent

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:03.369
and any word or paragraph as a sequence of numbers.

00:04:03.369 --> 00:04:06.009
And as we saw, these numbers can be stored

00:04:06.009 --> 00:04:08.529
as on or off electrical signals.

00:04:09.614 --> 00:04:19.374
Every word you see on every webpage on your phone is represented using a system like this.

00:04:19.598 --> 00:04:26.598
Now, let's consider photos, videos, and all the graphics you see on the screen.

00:04:26.598 --> 00:04:29.583
All of these images are made out of teeny tiny...

00:04:29.583 --> 00:04:33.663
dots called pixels, and each pixel has a color.

00:04:33.663 --> 00:04:37.783
Each of the colors can be represented with numbers.

00:04:37.783 --> 00:04:39.543
When you consider that a typical

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:42.327
has millions of these pixels.

00:04:42.327 --> 00:04:45.607
And a typical video shows 30 images per second.

00:04:45.607 --> 00:04:48.047
Now we're talking about a lot of data here.

00:04:49.552 --> 00:04:52.132
(upbeat music)

00:04:53.672 --> 00:04:57.952
Every sound is basically a series of vibrations in the air.

00:04:57.952 --> 00:04:59.536
Vibrations can be represented.

00:04:59.536 --> 00:05:06.536
graphically as a waveform. Any point on this waveform can be represented by a number.

00:05:06.536 --> 00:05:09.521
And this way any sound can be broken down.

00:05:09.521 --> 00:05:12.281
down into a series of numbers.

00:05:12.281 --> 00:05:18.401
If you want higher quality sound, you would pick 32-bit audio over 8-bit audio.

00:05:18.401 --> 00:05:19.506
More bits means a high quality sound.

00:05:19.506 --> 00:05:21.506
higher range of numbers.

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:33.330
But you will be dealing with images, or sound, or video.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:37.770
So if you want to understand how computers work on the inside, it all comes down to these

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.210
simple ones and zeros.

00:05:39.475 --> 00:05:43.315
and the electrical signals in the circuits behind them.

00:05:43.315 --> 00:05:46.435
They are the backbone of how all computers input,

00:05:46.435 --> 00:05:49.459
store, process, and output information.

00:05:49.459 --> 00:05:52.039
(upbeat music)
